        <div id="tm4500" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The LifeSmart TM4500: Discontinued, and What Replaced It</h2>
<p>
            The TM4500 PowerTouch is the LifeSmart most people search for, at about 720 searches a month. LifeSmart's
            product page now says plainly that it "has been discontinued and will not be restocked", and recommends the
            TM4000C as its direct replacement, with the same 3.5 HP brushless motor and PowerTouch fold for $500 less, at
            $1,199 against the TM4500's last $1,699.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The TM4500's figures, and the conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            LifeSmart and Costco's listing agree on a 20 by 60-inch two-ply belt and 0 to 14 mph. LifeSmart gives 10%
            incline and -3% decline and a 3.5 HP brushless motor; Sole's blog coverage of the Costco model also gives 3.5
            HP. The disagreements are about capacity and incline. Sole's blog gives 300 lb; a reseller listing gives 325
            lb in one place and 350 lb in its specification table; a retailer's LifeSmart brand guide gives 350 lb, a 22
            by 60-inch belt and -3% to 15% incline. LifeSmart's own page does not state a capacity for the TM4500. We
            therefore leave capacity off its card and suggest assuming 300 lb, the figure LifeSmart gives for the TM4000C
            that replaces it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying one now</h3>
          <p>
            Costco sold the TM4500 for $999.99, according to Sole's blog, and open-box and used units still circulate
            through liquidators and local listings. LifeSmart's warranty runs one year from the original purchase and is
            claimed through the retailer, so a second-hand TM4500 has effectively none. Pay well under the TM4000C's
            $1,199 for one, test the PowerTouch fold and sensors before paying, and check the console works fully. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costco-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>LifeSmart at Costco: TM6500, TM4800 and TM55</h2>
<p>
            Costco sells LifeSmart models you will not find on LifeSmart's own store, which is why model searches for the
            TM6500 and TM4800 are so common. Costco's pages would not show us prices on 5 November 2026, so the prices
            here come from other sources and dates, and we say which.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TM6500 PowerTouch</h3>
          <p>
            The TM6500 (Costco item 1975952) is the touchscreen model: a 22-inch HD screen that pivots and supports
            casting, a 4.0 HP brushless motor, up to 14 mph, a 20 by 60-inch belt, 30 programs and a 325 lb capacity, at
            231 lb. Costco's own page contradicts itself on incline, giving 10% with -3% decline in its highlights and 15%
            in its specification table. It is a Costco Direct, online-only item delivered to the door or a ground-floor
            room within about 50 miles of a warehouse, with paid White Glove assembly available.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TM4800</h3>
          <p>
            The TM4800 has been a Costco warehouse model. A Costco deal tracker recorded it at $999.99 and low stock at one
            warehouse in February 2026. An open-box retailer's listing gives a 20 by 60-inch belt, 10% incline and 3%
            decline, 30 programs, a 300 lb capacity and 197 lb, but no motor figure, and we found no Costco or LifeSmart
            page for it on the day. Treat it as a warehouse item that may or may not be in your local Costco, and confirm
            the motor rating on the box.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TM55</h3>
          <p>
            The TM55 (Costco item 2023152) is Costco's budget PowerTouch: a 2.75 HP brushless motor, a 20 by 55-inch belt,
            0 to 10 mph, 10% incline and -3% decline, a 300 lb capacity and 171 lb. A Slickdeals post showed it at
            $599.99 at an earlier date. It is a walking machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Costco's main advantage is its returns policy, which covers a treadmill that disappoints in a way LifeSmart's
            one-year warranty may not. Our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill at Costco</a> explains delivery, returns and the Costco-specific model numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The LifeSmart Warranty: One Year</h2>
<p>
            LifeSmart's warranty page states "a 1 year limited warranty on all products". It covers defects in materials
            and workmanship on functional parts for one year from purchase, for personal use only; cosmetic issues are
            not covered, the original purchaser or gift recipient must claim, and claims go through the place of purchase
            rather than LifeSmart. The TM3000's page repeats it as a one-year limited parts warranty. Extended plans are
            sold separately at checkout, from $49.99 for a year to $99.99 for five.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is short. The Bowflex T6, $999 on sale on the day we checked, has a lifetime motor warranty; Schwinn's
            $899 models carry 10 years on the frame and motor; Horizon gives lifetime frame and motor cover on its AT
            range. LifeSmart's brushless motors may well be durable — LifeSmart claims 20 times the life of a brushed
            motor, which we cannot verify — but if one fails in year two, the repair is yours. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how to weigh frame, motor, parts and labour terms, and when an extended plan is worth buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motors-decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Brushless Motors, Decline and Other Claims</h2>
<p>
            Every current LifeSmart PowerTouch model uses a brushless motor, rated from 2.75 HP (TM55) to 4.0 HP (TM5000
            and TM6500). LifeSmart does not say whether these are continuous or peak ratings, and on the TM3000, where an
            independent figure exists, it differs (3.0 HP at LifeSmart, 2.5 HP continuous at a retailer). Brushless motors
            have no brushes to wear out and tend to run quieter; our guides to <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower</a> and <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC vs DC motors</a> explain what the ratings mean.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most LifeSmart models offer -3% decline as well as incline, which is uncommon at this price. Decline lets you
            practise downhill running and work the muscles that brake you on descents; our guide to <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmill decline is good for</a> explains who benefits, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a> covers the alternatives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for LifeSmart on 5 November 2026. No
            LifeSmart treadmill has been recalled. The only LifeSmart recall, in February 2015, covered Lifepro-brand
            portable mini space heaters for an electrical shock hazard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>LifeSmart vs Similar Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            LifeSmart's prices put it against some well-established folding treadmills. On the day we checked:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bowflex T6, $999 on sale:</strong> a 20 by 60-inch deck, 15 levels of incline, 325 lb and a
            lifetime motor warranty, but a manual fold.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="/schwinn-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Schwinn SFT4.3</a>, $899:</strong> a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph and 12% incline, with 10 years on frame and motor.</li>
            <li><strong>Horizon 7.0 AT:</strong> a 20 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline and 325 lb with Horizon's long frame and
            motor cover.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of those folds itself, and none offers LifeSmart's decline. If the PowerTouch fold is the reason you are
            looking, a LifeSmart is one of very few options at the price. If it is not, the alternatives give you more
            protection for similar money. Our <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a> and <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $1,000</a> compare more of them, and our <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Dick's Sporting Goods treadmills</a> guide covers what that retailer carries.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is LifeSmart on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            Some LifeSmart models appear on Amazon US, including a TM2500 listing; the TM3000 and TM5000 cards link to
            their Amazon listings. The Costco-only TM6500 is not on Amazon, so its place in the cards goes to the closest
            big-screen folding treadmill that is, the NordicTrack T Series 16. We could not find the TM4000C or the
            TM4500 on Amazon US either, so their cards show the closest machines Amazon does sell: the ProForm Pro 2000,
            a 60-inch folding deck with decline at about the TM4000C’s price, and the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 in
            place of the discontinued TM4500. If you find a LifeSmart listing on Amazon, check the model number and the
            C suffix in the title, and that the seller is LifeSmart or Amazon, since warranty claims go through the place
            of purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a LifeSmart Treadmill</h2>
          <p>LifeSmart treadmills can suit the right buyer. These are the mistakes that make them a poor buy.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Not reading the warranty</h3>
              <p>Every LifeSmart carries a one-year limited warranty on functional parts. At $1,199 to $1,699, rivals offer lifetime or 10-year motor cover. If you buy a LifeSmart, buy it where returns are easy, such as Costco, or price in an extended plan.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Mixing up the C and non-C models</h3>
              <p>The TM4000 and TM4000C, and TM3000 and TM3000C, are different versions at the same price. LifeSmart says the TM4000C adds a reinforced incline frame, wireless charging and more programs. Check the exact model in the listing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a single capacity figure</h3>
              <p>TM4500 capacity is quoted as 300, 325 and 350 lb in different places, and the TM6500’s incline appears as both 10% and 15% on the same Costco page. Confirm figures on the box or with the seller before buying for a heavier user.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying near-new for a used TM4500</h3>
              <p>It is discontinued, and the warranty does not pass to a second owner. With the TM4000C new at $1,199, a used TM4500 is worth well under that.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">LifeSmart's appeal is PowerTouch, a fold that does the lifting for you, plus decline
          at a mid-range price — but every LifeSmart has a one-year warranty.</strong> The TM4500 is discontinued; its
          named replacement, the TM4000C at $1,199, is the model to buy if you want one. Costco's TM6500 adds a 22-inch
          touchscreen, but Costco's own page gives two incline figures for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the automatic fold is not essential, the <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Bowflex T6</a> at $999 on sale or a <a href="/schwinn-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Schwinn SFT4.3</a> at $899 gives a 60-inch deck with far longer motor cover.
          </p>
        </div>

